# feature-a-the-threadvar-area-is-3072-bytes-of-bss-in-every-program-that-has-no-threadvar
#
# A NilPy program gets a ZERO-byte per-thread variable area with no flag, so
# __pxxTlsBlockSize is TLS_USER_FIRST_OFF alone. Unlike the Pascal arm there is
# no text scan behind this and there is nothing for one to find: the language
# has no thread-local declaration, so no NilPy source can declare one however it
# is spelled.
#
# WHAT IT IS REALLY GUARDING is the Pascal RTL chain a NilPy program drags in.
# isNilPy is true for that whole compilation, so "this frontend has no such
# keyword" is NOT the same claim as "this compilation cannot declare one" -- and
# the import below puts a real Pascal unit on the chain on purpose. If any unit
# on it ever declares a threadvar, the Pascal allocator refuses at 0 bytes and
# this row fails to BUILD with the cap's own diagnostic, naming the unit, the
# line, and the flag that fixes it. The path is relative to THIS file's own
# directory, which is why it reads 'units/...' and not 'test/units/...'.
# Measured, not assumed: a NilPy program
# importing a .pas unit that declares a threadvar exits 1 at -dPXX_TLS_USER_0.
#
# The pinned compiler answers 4224 for this file, which is the positive control.
import 'units/utlsblock.pas'

print("block=%d" % TlsBlock())
